# Unit Testing Controller Rule

## Critical Rules

### Related Rules
This rule must be used in conjunction with:
- [Testing General Principles](.cursor/rules/manual/testing-general-principles.mdc) - For fundamental testing principles and practices
- [Unit Testing Best Practices](.cursor/rules/manual/unit-testing-best-practices.mdc) - For general unit testing best practices

### Controllers
**Guidelines:**
- Mock all dependencies injected into the controller (repositories, services, etc.).
- If the controller has only one repository, create a partial mocked repository and assign it to a protected property (e.g., `protected MockInterface $repository;`).
- If the controller has more than one repository, create multiple partial mocked repositories and assign them to separate protected properties.
- Always create a protected property for the controller (e.g., `protected Controller $controller;`) to reuse in all test cases.
- Implement a `setUp` method in each controller test file as follows:

```php
protected Controller $controller;
protected MockInterface $repository;

protected function setUp(): void
{
    parent::setUp();
    $this->repository = $this->partialMock(Repository::class);
    $this->controller = $this->app->make(Controller::class);
}
```

- If a controller method contains a FormRequest as an argument, use the `mockRequest` method from the `Tests\Traits\TestControllerAction` trait to create the request:

```php
use Tests\Traits\TestControllerAction;

// ...

use TestControllerAction;

public function testStore()
{
    $params = [];
    $request = $this->mockRequest(StoreRequest::class, $params);
    $this->repository->shouldReceive('create')
        ->with($params)
        ->once()
        ->andReturn(new Asset());

    $result = $this->app->call([$this->controller, 'store'], compact('request'));
    $this->assertInstanceOf(AssetItem::class, $result);
}
```

- Use Laravel's container to inject mocks.
- **Always use** `$this->app->call([$this->controller, 'method'], [...])` to invoke controller actions in tests, passing parameters as an associative array. This ensures Laravel resolves dependencies and route-model bindings as in real requests.
- Test each public method (action) for:
  - Success (happy path)
  - Validation errors
  - Not found (e.g., ModelNotFoundException)
  - Authorization (if applicable)
  - Error handling (e.g., exceptions, failed operations)
- Assert the structure and content of the response (status, data, error messages).
- Use `assertJson`, `assertStatus`, and similar helpers for API responses.

### Example Case
```php
namespace MetaFox\Storage\Tests\Unit\Http\Controllers\Api\v1;

use Tests\Traits\TestControllerAction;
use MetaFox\Storage\Http\Controllers\Api\v1\AssetAdminController as Controller;
use MetaFox\Storage\Repositories\AssetRepositoryInterface as Repository;

class AssetAdminControllerTest extends TestCase
{
    use TestControllerAction;

    protected Controller $controller;
    protected MockInterface $repository;

    protected function setUp(): void
    {
        parent::setUp();
        $this->repository =  $this->partialMock(Repository::class);
        $this->controller = $this->app->make(Controller::class);
    }

    public function testStore()
    {
        $params  = [];
        $request= $this->mockRequest(StoreRequest::class, $params);
        $this->repository->shouldReceive('create')
            ->with($params)
            ->once()
            ->andReturn(new Asset());

        $result = $this->app->call([$this->controller, 'store'], ['request'=>$request]);
        $this->assertInstanceOf(AssetItem::class, $result);
    }
}
```
